Project Overview

This project analyzes a banking credit-risk dataset containing 12,000 customer loan records and 22 features to explore loan characteristics, customer financial profiles, and patterns associated with loan risk.

The project combines Python-based exploratory data analysis with an interactive Power BI dashboard to transform the raw loan data into meaningful financial and risk insights.

Built Using
Power BI — Interactive dashboard and business intelligence reporting
Python — Data exploration, statistical analysis, and visualization
Pandas — Data manipulation and analysis
Matplotlib & Seaborn — Data visualization
Dataset Overview

The dataset contains information about customers, their financial characteristics, loan applications, and loan outcomes.

Key variables include:

Customer demographics
Annual income
Credit score
Existing loans
Dependents
Account balance
Loan amount
Loan purpose
Loan term
Interest rate
Debt-to-income ratio
Region
Loan status
Default risk flag
Application and approval dates

The dataset contains 12,000 records with no missing values or duplicate rows.

Exploratory Data Analysis

Python was used to understand the structure and characteristics of the dataset before developing the Power BI dashboard.

Data Quality Analysis
Checked dataset dimensions and data types
Identified missing values
Checked for duplicate records
Examined categorical variable distributions
Reviewed numerical variables using descriptive statistics
Identified unique identifier columns
Examined date fields for further analysis
Financial & Risk Analysis

The following relationships were explored:

Credit Score vs Loan Status
Debt-to-Income Ratio vs Loan Status
Annual Income vs Loan Amount
Financial variable correlations
Relationships among key financial variables using pair plots
Key EDA Findings
The dataset contains 12,000 loan records with no missing values or duplicate rows.
Loan_Status is highly imbalanced, with Default representing the overwhelming majority of records.
Credit scores for the Approved group are generally higher than those observed in the Default group, although the Approved group contains only a small number of observations.
Annual Income and Debt-to-Income Ratio show a moderately strong negative correlation (~ -0.61).
Loan Amount and Debt-to-Income Ratio show a moderate positive correlation (~ 0.48).
Annual Income and Loan Amount show almost no linear correlation (~ 0.02).
Credit Score and Interest Rate show very weak linear relationships with the other financial variables examined.
The Debt-to-Income Ratio contains unusually large values and therefore requires careful interpretation in the dashboard.
Dashboard Structure

The Power BI dashboard is being designed around practical credit-risk and portfolio questions rather than simply reproducing every Python visualization.

Page 1: Credit Risk Overview

The overview page will provide a high-level view of the loan portfolio, including metrics such as:

Total Loan Records
Total Loan Amount
Loan Status Distribution
Average Credit Score
Average Loan Amount
Average Interest Rate
Default/Risk Metrics
Page 2: Loan & Customer Analysis

This page will explore the characteristics of the customers and loans in the dataset.

Planned analysis includes:

Loan Amount by Loan Purpose
Loan Distribution by Region
Income Analysis
Credit Score Distribution
Employment Type
Education Level
Customer Demographics
Loan Term Analysis
Page 3: Credit Risk Analysis

This page focuses specifically on factors associated with loan risk.

Planned analysis includes:

Credit Score and Loan Status
Debt-to-Income Ratio Analysis
Risk by Loan Purpose
Risk by Region
Risk by Income Range
Risk by Credit Score Range
Risk-related financial indicators
Interactive Features

The dashboard will include interactive filters/slicers allowing users to explore the portfolio by dimensions such as:

Region
Loan Purpose
Employment Type
Education Level
Gender
Loan Status
